Sentence 2: "Phalaenopsis" is one of the most popular orchids in the trade, through the development of many artificial hybrids.
Sentence 3: The genus was a wastebasket taxon, and many of its members have been reclassified in smaller genera, most notably the Everlastings, now in the genus "Xerochrysum".
Sentence 4: They are native to Asia and northeastern Europe.
Sentence 5: The genus once included more species, but was split several decades ago into several genera, putting the economically important florist's chrysanthemums in the genus "Dendranthema".
Sentence 6: The only species found in the wild in North America is "Cephalanthera austiniae", the phantom orchid or snow orchid.
