Sentence 1: Elamite language
Elamite language Elamite is an extinct language that was spoken by the ancient Elamites.
Sentence 2: The last written records in Elamite appear around the conquest of the Achaemenid Empire by Alexander the Great.
Sentence 3: Elamite is generally thought to have no demonstrable relatives and is usually considered a language isolate.
Sentence 4: Elamite cuneiform, adapted from Akkadian cuneiform, was used from c. 2500 to 331 BC.
Sentence 5: Elamite cuneiform was largely a syllabary of some 130 glyphs at any one time and retained
Sentence 6: Elamite language
isolate, as it has no demonstrable relationship to the neighbouring Semitic languages, Indo-European languages, or to Sumerian, despite having adopted the Sumerian-Akkadian cuneiform script.
