Sentence 1: British Board of Film Classification
British Board of Film Classification The British Board of Film Classification (BBFC), previously the British Board of Film Censors, is a non-governmental organization, founded by the film industry in 1912 and responsible for the national classification and censorship of films exhibited at cinemas and video works (such as television programmes, trailers, adverts, public Information/campaigning films, menus, bonus content etc.)
Sentence 2: The BBFC was established in 1912 as the British Board of Film Censors by members of the film industry, who would rather manage their own censorship than have national or local government do it for them.
Sentence 3: The immediate impetus for the Board's formation stemmed from the furore surrounding the release in the UK in October 1912 of the film "From the Manger to the Cross", about the life of Jesus.
Sentence 4: The UK's film ratings are decided by the British Board of Film Classification and have been since 1912.
