Sentence 1: First Grammatical Treatise
First Grammatical Treatise The First Grammatical Treatise () is a 12th-century work on the phonology of the Old Norse or Old Icelandic language.
Sentence 2: It was given this name because it is the first of four grammatical works bound in the Icelandic manuscript "Codex Wormianus".
Sentence 3: This work is one of the earliest written works in Icelandic (and in any North Germanic language).
Sentence 4: The First Grammatical Treatise was of great interest to some mid-20th century linguists, since it systematically used the technique of minimal pairs to establish the inventory of distinctive sounds or phonemes in the Icelandic language, in a manner reminiscent of the methods of structural linguistics.
Sentence 5: It is also notable for revealing the existence of a whole series of nasal vowel phonemes, whose presence in the Icelandic language of the time would otherwise be unknown.
Sentence 6: First Grammatical Treatise
a major text showing the state of the language just prior to the writing of the Icelandic Sagas.
